        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Qin Shi Huang</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">259 BCE – 210 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">February 259 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, capital of the state of Zhao; son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> prince Yiren, later King Zhuangxiang of Qin, and Lady Zhao</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">July or August 210 BCE, at the palace in Shaqiu prefecture during his fifth tour of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, aged 49; the cause is unknown</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">King Zheng of Qin (from 246 BCE, with full power from 235 BCE); First Emperor, Shi Huangdi (221–210 BCE), founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">unifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE; creating the title huangdi, emperor; standardising weights, measures, coinage, axle lengths and script; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and counties in place of the feudal system; the northern walls that preceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>; the burning of books; his mausoleum and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army</a>; the search for an elixir of life</span></div>
