    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Between 230 and 221 BCE King Zheng of Qin conquered Han, Zhao, Yan, Wei, Chu and Qi, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>, and took the new title of huangdi, which Chinese monarchs used for the next two millennia.</li><li>With his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> he replaced the feudal order with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and counties and standardised weights, measures, coinage and script, while mobilising hundreds of thousands of workers for walls, roads, canals and his tomb.</li><li>Traditional historiography portrayed him as a brutal tyrant who burned books and buried scholars alive; scholars since the mid-20th century have questioned that picture, which stems partly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> that replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>After the emperor died on tour, the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a> hid the death for the two months' journey back to the capital, ordering carts of rotten fish to travel before and behind the imperial wagon to cover the smell of the body.</p></aside>
    </section>

